<h2>What Does the Cogito Actually Prove?</h2>

<p>Descartes arrives at <em>cogito ergo sum</em> — "I think, therefore I am" — as the one thing that survives his method of radical doubt. It is the foundation he has been looking for: something so certain that even an evil demon cannot make it false.</p>
<p>But exactly what has Descartes proven? Consider the following:</p>
<div>
<p>One reading: the cogito is a valid logical inference. "I am thinking" is the premise; "whatever thinks must exist" is the implicit second premise; "I exist" is the conclusion. The argument is airtight — if the premises are true, the conclusion must be.</p>
<p>A different reading: the cogito is not an inference at all. It is a direct, immediate intuition — the simultaneous recognition of thinking and existing in a single act of awareness. Descartes himself seems to prefer this reading. The "ergo" (therefore) does not indicate a logical step; it indicates an inseparable connection.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--concept">
  <span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span>
  <div class="callout__content">
    <p><strong>Question 1 — What does the cogito prove?</strong></p>
    <p>Does the cogito prove that a <em>self</em> exists — a unified, continuous thinker — or only that <em>thinking is occurring</em>? Is Descartes entitled to the "I" in "I think, therefore I am"? What would be lost, and what would be gained, if we replaced it with something like "There is thinking, therefore something exists"?</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--note">
  <span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8505;</span>
  <div class="callout__content">
    <p><strong>Question 2 — Is the cogito enough?</strong></p>
    <p>Even granting that the cogito succeeds, it seems to prove remarkably little on its own: that <em>I</em> exist, right now, as a thinking thing. Descartes then uses it as a springboard to prove God’s existence, the reliability of reason, and the existence of the external world. Is that springboard strong enough? Is the cogito really a foundation capable of bearing that weight, or does Descartes ask too much of it?</p>
  </div>
</div>
